  "runner_up_case": "The strongest honest case for FALSE rests on finality and the two dissenting items. Vindobona.org and ksl.com report that the Innsbruck court sentenced Benko to 30 months' unconditional imprisonment, and as of the as-of date (2026-10-01) the operative sentence is 30 months, not two years: krone.at states the retrial ended with 30 months 'taking into account the final judgment from October 2025', and bluewin.ch/reuters confirm the Supreme Court overturned part of the October 2025 verdict and a September 2026 retrial raised the term. If the claim is read as asserting the sentence Benko received is two years, it is materially wrong today. The case is reinforced by Q4 remaining open: no primary court document has been obtained, every supporting item is secondary press, the October 2025 verdict itself was mixed (conviction on one count, acquittal on another, later overturned), and press summaries could have conflated the prosecution's sentencing demand with the imposed term. The case ultimately fails, however, because the claim is a past-tense assertion about a specific October 2025 event: contemporaneous reporting (thelocal.at 2025-10-15, costar.com 2025-10-17) and Reuters' retrospective ('original two years') independently corroborate a two-year sentence imposed by the Innsbruck Regional Court in October 2025, while the 30-month reports describe the distinct September 2026 retrial proceeding, not the October 2025 one.",
  "would_flip_it": [
    "A primary record of the October 2025 ruling — written judgment, court press release, or APA/ORF report quoting the court — showing the imposed term was not two years' unconditional imprisonment (e.g., fully or partly suspended, a different length, or sentencing deferred to a later date).",
    "Credible evidence that the October 2025 hearing ended in acquittal or in no prison sentence, and that early reports mistook the prosecution's sentencing demand or a proposed penalty for the court's actual verdict.",
    "Credible evidence that the verdict was issued by a different court than the Innsbruck Regional Court (Landesgericht Innsbruck), or on a date outside October 2025.",
    "An appellate or Supreme Court ruling annulling the October 2025 conviction in its entirety, with reporting establishing that no two-year sentence was ever validly imposed — as opposed to the documented partial overturn of one acquittal that merely led to the September 2026 retrial."
  ]
}
